          #home — The home screen, and it is COVERED SOMEWHERE ELSE rather than uncovered: round-11.1/v1 "Constellation" is a WebGL canvas composition, so a computed-style walk can read nothing painted on it. tests/home.js measures it FROM THE PIXELS instead — 16 elements, worst 4.23:1, on the rendered frame — which check 14 already names. Two DOM checkers over one canvas would be one checker rotting.
          #inspector-scrim — The worker inspector's scrim, on the same footing as the rail's. The inspector ITSELF is walked (surface `inspector`, 146 nodes measured) — it is only the sheet beside it that carries nothing to measure.
          #rail-scrim — The rail's scrim on a narrow window — a translucent sheet with no text in it and no boundary of its own, so it is neither a text node nor a non-text INDICATOR under check 3's definition. What matters about a scrim is what can still be read THROUGH it, and check 8 measures exactly that on every run: a 12% sheet takes 4.54:1 down to 4.25:1 and the 4.25 is what gets reported, not the stylesheet's 4.54.
          #voice-panel — The voice panel mid-listen. Reaching it needs a live audio device: main.js hides the talk button entirely when voice_readiness refuses, and the panel only paints once start_voice_capture has a real stream. A browser fixture that forced it open would be measuring markup the product does not paint on a machine with no microphone.
